<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  AgroBenta DEBUG-ONLY network security configuration.

  This file deliberately shadows src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml in
  debug builds only, so that a physical Android device on the same Wi-Fi as the
  development machine can reach the plain-HTTP Docker stack:

      flutter run, with the API_BASE_URL dart-define set to
      http://PC-LAN-IP:8001/api

  ## Why the main config is not enough

  src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml permits cleartext for exactly
  three hosts, 10.0.2.2 (emulator), localhost and 127.0.0.1, and denies it
  everywhere else. A physical phone is none of those, so Android blocks the
  request before it leaves the device and the app reports
  "Could not reach the server" for what is really a cleartext-policy refusal.

  ## Why cleartext is allowed broadly here, and only here

  Android's <domain> elements match hostnames and IP literals. There is no CIDR
  or subnet syntax, so "any private LAN address" cannot be expressed, and the
  development machine's LAN IP is assigned by DHCP and changes from session to
  session. Hard-coding one IP into the main config would break on the next
  ipconfig run, which is why the main config is left strict instead.

  Rather than track a dynamic address, the permission is granted for the whole
  debug build type, which is the only build type that ever talks to a
  developer-controlled LAN over plain HTTP. Debug APKs are never distributed.

  This is NOT a production posture. Release and profile builds keep resolving
  @xml/network_security_config to src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml,
  which is deny-by-default, so production must be HTTPS. If a staging host
  genuinely cannot serve TLS, add it to the main config's <domain-config>
  with cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" and a comment saying so.

  See mobile/AGENTS.md and mobile/lib/core/config/app_config.dart.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
